WEBVTT

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:08.281
The compute subsystem runs general-purpose GPU work, from buffers

00:00:08.381 --> 00:00:12.918
and WGSL pipelines to ndarrays, kernels, and readback.

00:00:14.833 --> 00:00:18.205
Storage buffers hold the data that shaders read and

00:00:18.305 --> 00:00:22.221
write, and uniform buffers hold small blocks of parameters.

00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:26.357
Storage buffers accept writes by default; reading one

00:00:26.457 --> 00:00:30.469
back needs the copy-source flag, reported by canReadback.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.533
Writes are padded to four-byte alignment, so

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:35.770
a ten-byte write fills twelve bytes.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:41.348
A compute pipeline compiles your WGSL, with an

00:00:41.448 --> 00:00:44.853
automatic or an explicit bind group layout.

00:00:45.266 --> 00:00:48.494
Bind groups attach buffers to the shader's bindings,

00:00:48.594 --> 00:00:51.246
and dispatch launches a grid of workgroups.

00:00:51.666 --> 00:00:56.313
The one-, two-, and three-dimensional helpers divide the problem size by

00:00:56.413 --> 00:01:00.334
the workgroup size and round up, so every element is covered.

00:01:00.766 --> 00:01:04.353
Dispatches can also be encoded into your own command

00:01:04.453 --> 00:01:07.685
encoder, or batched into a single compute pass.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.567
ndarrays describe memory with a dtype, a

00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:15.558
shape, byte strides, and a byte offset.

00:01:15.966 --> 00:01:19.609
Strides are measured in bytes, so an element's offset is

00:01:19.709 --> 00:01:22.885
the base offset plus each index times its stride.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:27.711
A CPUndarray lives in WebAssembly memory, and destroy

00:01:27.811 --> 00:01:31.626
frees its data, shape, and stride allocations.

00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:36.638
A GPUndarray lives in a storage buffer: empty allocates

00:01:36.738 --> 00:01:40.573
one it owns, and wrap borrows an existing one.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:47.538
Upload and readback move arrays between the two residences, and the

00:01:47.638 --> 00:01:52.889
f32, u32, and i32 dtypes map directly to WGSL scalars.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:00.496
The kernels library provides parallel building blocks: element-wise

00:02:00.596 --> 00:02:04.217
math, dot products, and GEMM over f32, u32,

00:02:04.317 --> 00:02:08.716
and c64, plus reductions, scans, sorts, and solvers.

00:02:09.133 --> 00:02:12.294
Reductions combine values in a tree inside each

00:02:12.394 --> 00:02:16.457
workgroup, and arg-reductions also return the winning index.

00:02:16.866 --> 00:02:21.891
An exclusive scan gives each element the sum of everything before it, and

00:02:21.991 --> 00:02:26.174
compaction uses a scan of keep flags to pack the kept values.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.935
Radix sorts order u32 keys, or key-value

00:02:30.035 --> 00:02:33.198
pairs, stably, using one pass per bit.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.972
GEMM computes alpha times A times B, plus beta

00:02:37.072 --> 00:02:42.332
times the output, staging sixteen-by-sixteen tiles in workgroup memory.

00:02:42.733 --> 00:02:47.058
Batched LU factorization with partial pivoting, and the matching

00:02:47.158 --> 00:02:50.377
solve, handle many small linear systems at once.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:56.644
Results return to JavaScript through a readback ring, which keeps

00:02:56.744 --> 00:03:00.541
three staging buffers by default and reuses them in turn.

00:03:00.966 --> 00:03:04.496
Each read copies a range into the next slot, maps it

00:03:04.596 --> 00:03:08.824
asynchronously, and resolves a typed array or a single scalar.

00:03:09.233 --> 00:03:11.952
For images, a blitter draws a packed

00:03:12.052 --> 00:03:14.616
RGBA8 buffer straight to a canvas.

00:03:16.533 --> 00:03:22.803
An f64 array can be stored, copied, and read back without loss,

00:03:22.903 --> 00:03:28.870
but WGSL has no runtime f64 type, so shaders compute in f32.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:35.405
Lifetimes are explicit: destroy releases buffers, readback rings, and CPU

00:03:35.505 --> 00:03:40.506
allocations, and a GPUndarray destroys only buffers it owns.

00:03:42.433 --> 00:03:46.318
To recap: buffers, pipelines and dispatch, ndarrays with

00:03:46.418 --> 00:03:50.589
byte strides, a kernels library, and ring-buffered readback.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.060
The full reference is in the Compute section of the documentation.
